  /**
   * Root
   * @description Basic status — no auth required.  Matches Rails ``root :to => 'api#status'``.
   *
   * Rails' action is a DB liveness check, not a static payload
   * (api_controller.rb)::
   *
   *     Org.connection.execute('SELECT 1').to_a
   *   rescue Exception => e
   *     raise ApiError.new(:internal_server_error, "service connection failed: #{e.message}")
   *
   * This returned ``{"status": "up"}`` unconditionally, so a pod with an
   * unreachable database reported itself healthy while Rails reported 500 — the
   * single most misleading answer a status endpoint can give.
   *
   * The body deliberately says only that the service is down. Rails interpolates
   * the driver text (``"service connection failed: #{e.message}"``); this port
   * sanitises database errors so SQL, schema and driver internals never reach a
   * client (errors.py DATABASE_ERROR_MESSAGE, middleware/_error_handling.py:21),
   * and an unauthenticated endpoint is the last place to start leaking them —
   * this one answers before any credential is checked. The cause is logged with a
   * full traceback, which is where an operator should read it.
   *
   * ``from None`` is load-bearing: the HTTPException handler walks the exception
   * CHAIN (``contains_database_error``) and would otherwise swap this message for
   * the generic sanitised one, so chaining would change the body and gain nothing.
   *
   * Only ``/`` and ``/api/v1`` gained the check. ``/health`` stays database-free
   * because that is what the container HEALTHCHECK probes (Dockerfile:100) — a
   * database blip should not restart the pod.
   */
  root: {
    responses: {
      /** @description Successful Response */
      200: {
        content: {
          "application/json": {
            [key: string]: string;
          };
        };
      };
    };
  };
